<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Der Blaue Reiter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as The Blue Rider, Blue Rider</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Der Blaue Reiter, &quot;the Blue Rider&quot;, was a group of artists formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1911 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-marc">Franz Marc</a>, joined by painters such as August Macke, Gabriele Münter and Paul Klee. Its exhibitions and its almanac of 1912 promoted expressive colour and abstraction and made Munich a centre of modern art. The group broke up with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, in which Macke and Marc were killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/der-blaue-reiter.jpg" alt="Der Blaue Reiter" width="205" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wassily Kandinsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BlaueReiter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1900 Munich was a leading art city: the Munich Secession was founded in 1892, the magazine Jugend gave its name to German Art Nouveau, and the <b>Blue Rider</b> group of Wassily Kandinsky and Franz Marc formed there in 1911.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Johannes Itten, influenced by Franz Cižek, Friedrich Wilhelm August Fröbel and <b>Der Blaue Reiter</b>, brought an Expressionist spirit.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Die Brücke was formed in Dresden in 1905 and <b>Der Blaue Reiter</b> in Munich in 1911, the year Kandinsky painted what he called the first abstract painting and Cubism caused a scandal at the Salon des Indépendants.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
